# Connecting a real MyChart record.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and restart the dev server — Vite reads env
# only at startup. Without these the app still runs: the connect panel says it is
# not configured and the explorer shows the de-identified sample record.
#
# These are VITE_-prefixed because they are inlined into the client bundle at
# BUILD time, not read at runtime. A PKCE client id is public by design (there is
# no client secret in this flow), so this is safe — but it does mean a deployed
# build needs them set when `pnpm build` runs, not as a Worker secret afterwards.

# There is no global "sandbox mode". Which client id is used follows the
# provider you pick in the app's dropdown:
#
#   UCSF Health / Sutter Health  ->  VITE_EPIC_CLIENT_ID
#   Epic Sandbox                 ->  VITE_EPIC_SANDBOX_CLIENT_ID
#
# The dropdown defaults to UCSF, so to test against Epic's sandbox you must
# select "Epic Sandbox" there. Setting only the sandbox id is fine: the
# production organizations are then shown as unconfigured.

# Production organizations (UCSF, Sutter). Requires Epic app review and the
# health system to enable your app.
VITE_EPIC_CLIENT_ID=your-epic-production-client-id

# Epic's non-production id, issued immediately on registration. Works against
# the Epic Sandbox provider with Epic's test patients — start here.
VITE_EPIC_SANDBOX_CLIENT_ID=your-epic-non-production-client-id

# Overrides the default scope: openid fhirUser launch/patient patient/*.rs
# VITE_EPIC_SCOPE=
